"""CySA+ Domain 2 drill content — objectives 2.1 to 2.5.

Original prose written against CompTIA's published CS0-003 objectives
(build/cysa-spine.json). Class decks and transcripts are read for scope
only; no instructor wording or Knowledge Check questions are reproduced,
because the repo is public.

Shapes:
    FLASHCARDS  (objective, front, back)
    QUIZ        (objective, question, [4 choices], answer_index, explanation)
    SCENARIOS   (objective, situation, question, [4 choices], answer_index, explanation)

Recovered 2026-10-25 from the published page (d2.frag.html) after the session that
built it ended without committing its source. drill_page.py regenerates that
page byte for byte from this file, which is the proof the recovery is lossless.
"""

FLASHCARDS = [
 ('2.1',
  'Credentialed versus non-credentialed scanning — what changes?',
  'A credentialed scan authenticates to the host and reads installed versions, patch state and configuration directly. A non-credentialed scan infers from the outside. Credentialed is far more accurate and finds far more, with fewer false positives; non-credentialed shows you roughly what an unauthenticated attacker sees.'),
 ('2.1',
  'Agent-based versus agentless scanning — what is the trade-off?',
  'An agent runs on the host, so it reports even when the device is off the corporate network and imposes no scan traffic. Agentless needs no software deployed and no endpoint footprint, but only sees hosts that are reachable and online when the scan runs. Roaming laptops are the usual argument for agents.'),
 ('2.1',
  'Passive versus active scanning.',
  'Active scanning sends traffic and provokes responses — accurate, but it generates load and can disturb fragile systems. Passive scanning observes traffic already on the wire and identifies hosts and services from it. Passive is safe on sensitive networks and finds only what happens to talk.'),
 ('2.1',
  'Internal versus external scanning — why run both?',
  'They answer different questions. An external scan shows the attack surface exposed to the internet. An internal scan shows what an attacker reaches once inside, or what an insider can touch. A clean external scan says nothing about lateral movement.'),
 ('2.1',
  'Static versus dynamic application analysis.',
  'Static analysis inspects source or binaries without running them, so it can cover every path including ones rarely executed. Dynamic analysis tests the running application and sees real behavior and configuration. Static finds flaws earlier and cheaper; dynamic proves exploitability.'),
 ('2.1',
  'What is fuzzing, and what is it good at finding?',
  'Feeding malformed, unexpected or random input to an application to see what breaks. It is a dynamic technique, and it is strong at finding input-handling failures — overflows, crashes, unhandled exceptions — precisely because it tries things no reasonable developer anticipated.'),
 ('2.1',
  'What is reverse engineering used for in vulnerability work?',
  'Understanding a binary when source is unavailable: how a protocol works, what a malware sample does, or whether a vendor product handles input safely. It is static analysis on something you cannot read directly.'),
 ('2.1',
  'What do map scans and device fingerprinting contribute to asset discovery?',
  'A map scan establishes what is reachable — the hosts and open ports on a network. Fingerprinting identifies what each one actually is: operating system, version, service. Together they answer "what do we have", which is the precondition for knowing what needs patching.'),
 ('2.1',
  'Why is scan scheduling a security decision rather than an operational one?',
  'Because scan timing determines whether findings are current and whether production survives the scan. Scanning a transaction system at month-end close can degrade it; scanning only quarterly means acting on a stale picture. The window is a negotiation between accuracy and availability.'),
 ('2.1',
  'What makes scanning OT, ICS and SCADA environments different?',
  'Those devices were built for determinism and uptime, not for being probed, and many predate modern network stacks. An ordinary active scan can crash a PLC or disrupt a physical process. Passive monitoring and vendor-approved methods are the norm, and safety outranks completeness.'),
 ('2.1',
  'What is security baseline scanning?',
  'Measuring systems against a defined secure configuration rather than against a list of known CVEs. It answers "is this host configured as we decided it should be", which catches drift and misconfiguration that a vulnerability scan alone would miss.'),
 ('2.1',
  'What do CIS Benchmarks provide?',
  'Consensus-built, prescriptive configuration guidance for specific products — operating systems, databases, browsers, cloud services — with concrete settings. They are what baseline scans are usually measured against.'),
 ('2.1',
  'Which standard governs cardholder data, and what does it demand of scanning?',
  'PCI DSS. It is a contractual standard imposed by the card brands, and it requires both internal and external vulnerability scanning on a defined cadence, with external scans performed by an Approved Scanning Vendor. Failing a scan has commercial consequences, not just security ones.'),
 ('2.1',
  'What is the ISO 27000 series, and how does it differ from CIS benchmarks?',
  'A family of standards for information security management — 27001 being the certifiable management-system standard. It governs how security is organized and governed, where CIS benchmarks specify how a particular system is configured. One is management, the other is settings.'),
 ('2.1',
  'How does network segmentation affect scanning?',
  'It constrains what a scanner can see. A scanner in one segment reports only that segment unless routes and firewall rules permit otherwise, so segmented environments need scanners placed per segment or explicit allowances. Otherwise coverage gaps look like clean results.'),
 ('2.1',
  'What is a sensitivity level in scan configuration, and why does it matter?',
  'How aggressive the scan is permitted to be — which checks run, how much traffic, whether potentially disruptive tests are included. Set too low and you miss findings; set too high on a fragile system and you cause the outage you were trying to prevent.'),
 ('2.2',
  'What does a vulnerability scanner actually report, and what does it not?',
  'It reports that a condition matching a signature was observed — a version, a setting, a response — with a severity and a reference. It does not report that the finding is exploitable in your environment, reachable by an attacker, or worth fixing first. Those are analyst judgments the tool cannot make.'),
 ('2.2',
  'What are network scanning and mapping tools for?',
  'Establishing what exists and what is listening: live hosts, open ports, running services and their versions, and the topology between them. The output is the inventory everything else depends on.'),
 ('2.2',
  'What does a web application scanner do that a general vulnerability scanner does not?',
  'It exercises the application — crawling pages, submitting input, following authentication — to find flaws in application logic and handling such as injection, cross-site scripting and broken access control. A general scanner examines the host and its services, not the app running on them.'),
 ('2.2',
  'What is a debugger used for in assessment work?',
  'Watching a program execute instruction by instruction: inspecting memory, registers and control flow. In vulnerability work it is how you confirm a crash is exploitable rather than merely a crash, and how you understand what a binary does.'),
 ('2.2',
  'What do cloud infrastructure assessment tools look at that a host scanner misses?',
  'The control plane — identity and access policies, storage permissions, network security groups, logging configuration, key management. Most serious cloud findings are misconfigurations in how resources are permissioned, not unpatched software inside them.'),
 ('2.2',
  'Two tools report different severities for the same finding. What should the analyst do?',
  'Treat severity as a vendor opinion and re-derive it from context: is it reachable, is it exploitable, what is the asset worth, is there a compensating control. Vendors weight differently and some inflate. The score is an input, not the answer.'),
 ('2.3',
  'What are the CVSS base metrics?',
  'Attack Vector, Attack Complexity, Privileges Required, User Interaction, Scope, and the three impact metrics — Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability. They describe the intrinsic characteristics of the vulnerability, independent of any particular environment.'),
 ('2.3',
  'What does Attack Vector measure, and which value is most severe?',
  'How close the attacker must be: Network, Adjacent, Local, or Physical. Network is most severe because it means remotely exploitable across the internet with no proximity requirement. Physical is least, because it requires the attacker to touch the device.'),
 ('2.3',
  'What does Scope mean in CVSS, and why does Changed raise the score so much?',
  'Scope describes whether exploiting the vulnerability affects resources beyond the vulnerable component. Changed means the blast radius crosses a security boundary — a container escape, a hypervisor break — so the impact is no longer confined to the thing that was vulnerable.'),
 ('2.3',
  'Why is a CVSS base score alone a poor way to prioritize?',
  'Because it deliberately ignores your environment. It does not know whether the host is internet-facing or isolated, whether the asset is a lab box or the payment system, whether an exploit exists in the wild, or whether a compensating control is already in place. Base score ranks severity; context ranks urgency.'),
 ('2.3',
  'Define true positive, false positive, true negative and false negative in scanning.',
  'True positive — reported and real. False positive — reported but not real, which costs analyst time. True negative — correctly not reported. False negative — real but not reported, which is the dangerous one, because nothing tells you it happened.'),
 ('2.3',
  'Why is a false negative more dangerous than a false positive?',
  'A false positive wastes effort and is self-correcting once investigated. A false negative produces a clean report over a real exposure, and nothing in the process will surface it. It is the reason scanning is not the same as assurance.'),
 ('2.3',
  'How does context — internal, external, isolated — change priority?',
  'An external-facing host is reachable by anyone and ranks highest. An internal host requires a foothold first. An isolated system, genuinely segmented with no route in, may be lower priority than its score suggests. Same CVSS, three different urgencies.'),
 ('2.3',
  'What does exploitability or weaponization add to prioritization?',
  'Whether anyone can actually use it. A high-scoring flaw with no public exploit is a theoretical risk; a medium-scoring one with working exploit code in a common toolkit is being used this week. Weaponized and in the wild outranks a higher base score.'),
 ('2.3',
  'Why does asset value belong in prioritization?',
  'Because identical vulnerabilities on different hosts carry different consequences. The same flaw on a disposable test VM and on the domain controller are not the same problem. Value includes the data held, the dependencies on it, and what its loss would cost.'),
 ('2.3',
  'What is a zero-day, and what can be done about one?',
  'A vulnerability with no vendor patch available — often disclosed or exploited before the vendor knew. You cannot patch it, so the response is compensating: virtual patching, access restriction, detection tuned to the technique, or taking the exposure offline until a fix exists.'),
 ('2.4',
  'Reflected versus persistent cross-site scripting.',
  'Reflected XSS is delivered in the request and echoed back in the response, so each victim must be induced to follow a crafted link. Persistent XSS is stored by the application and served to everyone who views it. Persistent is more severe because it needs no per-victim delivery.'),
 ('2.4',
  'What actually prevents cross-site scripting?',
  'Context-aware output encoding — encoding data for the place it is rendered — backed by input validation and a content security policy. Input filtering alone fails because the same input is safe in one context and dangerous in another.'),
 ('2.4',
  'What is the single most effective control against SQL injection?',
  'Parameterized queries, also called prepared statements. The query structure is fixed and user data can only ever be a value, never executable syntax. Escaping and blocklists are patchable workarounds; parameterization removes the class of flaw.'),
 ('2.4',
  'Distinguish buffer, stack, heap and integer overflow.',
  "A buffer overflow writes past the end of an allocated region. Stack overflow targets the call stack, where overwriting a return address can redirect execution. Heap overflow corrupts dynamically allocated memory and its metadata. Integer overflow is arithmetic exceeding the type's range, often producing an undersized allocation that then overflows."),
 ('2.4',
  'What mitigates overflow vulnerabilities?',
  'Bounds checking and memory-safe languages first. Then platform defenses — ASLR randomizes layout, DEP/NX marks data non-executable, stack canaries detect overwrites before return. These raise exploitation cost; they do not fix the underlying bug.'),
 ('2.4',
  'What is broken access control, and why is it so commonly found?',
  'Authenticated users reaching data or functions they should not — changing an identifier in a URL, calling an admin endpoint directly. It is common because authorization has to be enforced per object on the server for every request, and it is easy to enforce only in the interface.'),
 ('2.4',
  'What falls under cryptographic failures?',
  'Sensitive data not encrypted at all, encrypted weakly, or protected with broken algorithms or poor key management — hardcoded keys, reused IVs, plaintext in transit. The failure is usually in how crypto is used rather than in the algorithm.'),
 ('2.4',
  'What is CSRF, and what stops it?',
  "Cross-site request forgery makes an authenticated user's browser submit an action they did not intend, relying on cookies being sent automatically. Anti-CSRF tokens tied to the session, SameSite cookie attributes, and re-authentication for sensitive actions stop it."),
 ('2.4',
  'How does SSRF differ from CSRF?',
  "CSRF abuses the user's browser to act on the application. SSRF makes the *server* issue requests the attacker chooses — often to internal addresses the attacker cannot reach, including cloud metadata endpoints. The names are similar and the mechanisms are opposite."),
 ('2.4',
  'What is directory traversal, and what prevents it?',
  'Manipulating a path parameter to escape the intended directory and read files elsewhere. Prevention is canonicalizing the path and validating it resolves inside the permitted root, rather than filtering for traversal sequences, which has many encodings.'),
 ('2.4',
  'Local versus remote file inclusion.',
  'LFI makes the application include a file already on the server — sometimes a log file the attacker has poisoned. RFI makes it include a file from an attacker-controlled remote location. Both typically lead to code execution; RFI requires outbound fetching to be permitted.'),
 ('2.4',
  'What is insecure design, and why can it not be patched?',
  'A flaw in what was designed rather than in how it was built — a password reset with no rate limit, a workflow with no authorization step. There is no defective code to fix; the control was never specified. This is what threat modeling exists to catch.'),
 ('2.4',
  'What is security misconfiguration?',
  'Defaults left in place, unnecessary features enabled, verbose errors exposed, permissions too broad. The software is working as written; it was deployed wrong. Baseline scanning and hardening standards are the controls.'),
 ('2.4',
  'Why are end-of-life components a distinct category of risk?',
  'Because no fix is coming. Every vulnerability found from now on is permanent, and disclosure makes exploitation easier over time. The treatment is replacement, or isolation plus compensating controls if replacement is genuinely impossible.'),
 ('2.4',
  'What is data poisoning?',
  'Corrupting the data a system learns from or relies on, so that its outputs are wrong by design — tampering with training data, telemetry or a feed. The system functions correctly on bad input, which is why it is hard to detect by testing the system itself.'),
 ('2.4',
  'What distinguishes privilege escalation from remote code execution?',
  'RCE gets an attacker arbitrary execution on a host, usually at the privilege of the exploited service. Privilege escalation takes whatever access exists and raises it. Chained together they are the common path: RCE for a foothold, escalation for control.'),
 ('2.4',
  'What falls under identification and authentication failures?',
  'Weak or absent credential controls — permitted weak passwords, no protection against credential stuffing or brute force, exposed session identifiers, sessions that never expire, recovery flows that can be abused. The authentication exists but can be defeated.'),
 ('2.5',
  'What is a compensating control, and what makes one legitimate?',
  'A measure that reduces risk when the intended control cannot be implemented. It is legitimate when it addresses the same risk, is documented and approved, has an owner, and has an end date. Without those it is an excuse rather than a control.'),
 ('2.5',
  'Name the managerial, operational and technical control categories.',
  'Managerial controls are policy and process — risk assessments, standards. Operational controls are executed by people — training, awareness, procedures. Technical controls are implemented by systems — encryption, access control, logging. The categories describe who or what enforces them.'),
 ('2.5',
  'Distinguish preventative, detective, responsive and corrective controls.',
  'Preventative stops it happening. Detective notices it happened. Responsive acts while it is happening. Corrective restores afterwards and prevents recurrence. The same technology can occupy more than one category depending on how it is used.'),
 ('2.5',
  'What are the four stages of a sound patching process?',
  'Testing in a representative environment, implementation through change control, validation that the patch applied and the system works, and a rollback path if it does not. Skipping test and rollback is how patching causes the outage it was meant to prevent.'),
 ('2.5',
  'What is a maintenance window for?',
  'A pre-agreed period in which disruptive change may occur, so that availability expectations and security work do not collide unplanned. Its existence is what lets patching be scheduled rather than negotiated each time.'),
 ('2.5',
  'What makes a vulnerability exception acceptable?',
  'A documented business justification, a named owner, a defined expiry, compensating controls in the meantime, and approval at a level matching the risk. Exceptions are a managed decision to carry risk — an exception with no expiry is just an unfixed vulnerability with paperwork.'),
 ('2.5',
  'Name the four risk treatments and give an example of each.',
  'Accept — document and carry it. Transfer — insurance or a contractual shift to a third party. Avoid — stop doing the activity that creates the risk. Mitigate — apply controls to reduce likelihood or impact. Most real programs use all four across different risks.'),
 ('2.5',
  'What is a service-level objective in vulnerability management?',
  'A committed timeframe for remediation, usually tiered by severity — critical within so many days, high within more. It converts "we take security seriously" into something measurable, and it is what prioritization and escalation are measured against.'),
 ('2.5',
  'When should a vulnerability be escalated?',
  'When the SLO will be missed, when the owner will not act, when a compensating control cannot be arranged, or when newly weaponized exploit activity changes the urgency. Escalation is a defined path to someone who can authorize a trade-off, not a complaint.'),
 ('2.5',
  'What is attack surface management?',
  'The continuous practice of discovering everything exposed, assessing it, and reducing it. Continuous matters: the attack surface changes whenever anything is deployed, and the dangerous assets are usually the ones nobody remembered were there.'),
 ('2.5',
  'Edge discovery and passive discovery — what are they?',
  'Edge discovery finds what is exposed at the perimeter, including assets nobody has inventoried. Passive discovery identifies assets from existing data — traffic, DNS, certificate transparency — without probing. Both target the same problem: shadow assets that no scan covers because nobody knew to scan them.'),
 ('2.5',
  'How does penetration testing differ from adversary emulation?',
  'A penetration test finds and demonstrates exploitable weaknesses, usually broadly and within a scope. Adversary emulation replicates the techniques of a specific threat actor to test whether your detection and response notice them. One tests the walls; the other tests the guards.'),
 ('2.5',
  'What is a bug bounty program, and what does it require to work?',
  'Paying external researchers for responsibly disclosed vulnerabilities. It needs clear scope, a safe-harbor commitment, defined reward criteria, and the internal capacity to triage and fix what arrives — otherwise it generates findings nobody actions.'),
 ('2.5',
  'Give concrete examples of attack surface reduction.',
  'Decommissioning unused systems, closing unnecessary ports, removing unused services and accounts, limiting administrative access, retiring end-of-life components, consolidating redundant exposure. It is the cheapest form of risk reduction because removed things cannot be exploited.'),
 ('2.5',
  'Name the secure coding practices the objectives call out.',
  'Input validation, output encoding, session management, authentication, data protection, and parameterized queries. Together they cover the common failure modes: trusting input, rendering unsafely, mishandling identity and state, and building queries from strings.'),
 ('2.5',
  'What does a secure SDLC change about how vulnerabilities are handled?',
  'It moves security earlier — requirements, design review, threat modeling, secure coding standards, testing in the pipeline — so that flaws are found when they are cheap. Finding the same flaw in production costs far more than finding it in design.'),
 ('2.5',
  'What is threat modeling, and when is it done?',
  'Systematically asking what can go wrong with a design, who would attempt it, and what prevents it — during design, before the code exists. It is the one activity that reliably catches insecure design, because no test can find a control that was never specified.'),
]

